<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.17.2: Modify Expressions to Translate Graphs</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02046</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.17.2: Modify Expressions to Translate Graphs</md:title>
    <md:uuid>2e2eecd0-2648-4920-8b2f-c6d73f2af7fa</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <note id="note-00001">
        <para id="para-00001">Access the <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b62c9150250bf52d6cc37569213ab516e0090dca">Desmos guide PDF</link> for tips on solving problems with the Desmos graphing calculator.</para></note>
      
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
        <item>How would you change the equation \(y=x^2\) so that the vertex of the graph of the new equation is located at the following coordinates and the graph opens as described?</item>
      </list>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00002">\((0,11)\), opens up</para>
            <para id="para-00003">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00004">Compare your Answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00005">\(y=x^2+11\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00002">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00006">\((7,11)\), opens up</para>
            <para id="para-00007">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00008">Compare your Answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00009"><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=(x−7)^2+11\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00003">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00010">\((7,-3)\), opens down</para>
            <para id="para-00011">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00012">Compare your Answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00013"><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=-1(x−7)^2−3\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      <media alt="">
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/></media>
        <exercise id="exer-00004">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00014">Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Verify your predictions.</para>
            <para id="para-00015">Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00016">Compare your Answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00017">
                      <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_19_03_01.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00002">
        <item>Kiran graphed the equation \(y=x^2+1\) and noticed that the vertex is at \((0,1)\). He changed the equation to \(y=(x−3)^2+1\) and saw that the graph shifted 3 units to the right and the vertex is now at \((3,1)\).
    
    Next, he graphed the equation \(y=x^2+2x+1\) and observed that the vertex is at \((-1,0)\). Kiran thought, "If I change the squared term \(x^2\) to \((x−5)^2\), the graph will move 5 units to the right and the vertex will be at \((4,0)\)."
 </item>
      </list>
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00002">
        <title>Activity</title>
        <exercise id="a6a19eb6-3012-4617-a5d6-28ceacee7df9" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00018">Do you agree with Kiran?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00019">Correct! Disagree</para>
            <para id="para-00020">Incorrect. The correct answer is Disagree.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00005">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00021">Explain or show your reasoning why you agree or disagree with Kiran.</para>
            <para id="para-00022">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00023">Compare your Answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00024">You should disagree with Kiran.</para>
            <para id="para-00025">
                      <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_19_03_02.png"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <list id="list-00003">
              <item>The vertex of the graph of the original equation \(y=x^2+2x+1\) is at \((-1,0)\). The vertex of \(y=(x−5)^2+2x+1\) is at \((4,10)\).</item>
              <item>Evaluating \(y=(x−5)^2+2x+1\) at \(x = 4\) gives \(y=10\), not \(y=0\).</item>
              <item>The original equation is not in vertex form, so changing \(x^2\) into \((x−5)^2\) does not change the location of the vertex 5 units to the right.</item>
            </list>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Q4a-->
      
        <exercise id="exer-00006">
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            <para id="para-00026">\(y=(x+4)^2-1\) is a parabola with a vertex at \((-4,1)\) that opens upward.</para>
            <para id="para-00027">If we multiply \(y\) by 2, we get \(2y=2[(x+4)^2-1] = 2(x+4)^2-2\). How does this change the graph?</para>
            <para id="para-00028">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00007">
            <para id="para-00029">Compare your Answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00030">The parabola is narrower and the vertex is lower. The \(x\)-intercepts stay the same.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--end question-->
      <!--Q4b-->
      
        <exercise id="exer-00007">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00031">If we multiply \(y\) by \(\frac{1}{2}\), we get \(y=\frac{1}{2}[(x+4)^2-1] =\frac{1}{2}(x+4)^2-\frac{1}{2}.\) How does this change the graph?</para>
            <para id="para-00032">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00033">Compare your Answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00034">The parabola is wider and the vertex is higher. The \(x\)-intercepts stay the same.
</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--end question-->
      <!--BEGIN ADD DESMOS INTERACTIVE-->
      <!--START QUESTION # 5-->
